  "track": "track-10",
  "title": "unit tests assertions fixtures mocks stubs fakes and isolation",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "A unit test exercises one small behavior through its public contract and makes failures easy to diagnose; a unit is a design boundary, not necessarily one method or class.",
    "A strong test states expected behavior, supplies necessary inputs, executes the behavior, and checks an observable result. Arrange-Act-Assert and Given-When-Then are organizing conventions rather than framework requirements.",
    "A fixture is the context needed to run tests. Per-test setup promotes isolation; shared fixtures can save expensive setup but introduce order dependence, races, and hidden coupling.",
    "Parameterized tests apply one behavior check to deliberate inputs. Test doubles replace collaborators for specific purposes: stubs supply answers, mocks verify interactions, fakes provide lightweight working implementations, and spies record calls.",
    "Prefer observable state assertions. Verify interactions only when they are part of the contract; excessive interaction checks couple tests to implementation details.",
    "Tests should run independently in any order and in parallel. Shared mutable state, clocks, real network dependencies, unseeded randomness, and incomplete cleanup weaken repeatability."
  ],
